======================================================================================================================
L204 THE BOOSTED METRIC EXPANSION: does the tilt reach the solar system, and which bound binds
======================================================================================================================
  [PASS] V1 [THE STRUCTURAL FACT: the tilt generates no background MOND gradient] because the background scalar depends only on the clock, its gradient is parallel to the clock's normal, so the MOND invariant Y vanishes in the boosted frame exactly as it does at rest -- at every order in the velocity, not just to leading order
           (Y in the boosted frame simplifies to 0, identically in w; the boost tilts the normal and the scalar's gradient together, so nothing orthogonal is generated)
  [PASS] V2 [and the clock rate is not merely nearly invariant, it is exactly invariant] s is a scalar built from the clock's gradient, so the boost leaves it untouched at every order in the velocity, not only to second order as one might expect from time dilation. Taken with V1 the entire background clock sector is blind to the motion, so there is nothing at background level for the preferred frame to act upon
           (s = s_0 exactly, with no dependence on w whatsoever; the naive expectation of a second-order time dilation of 1.52e-06 does not appear because s is a scalar)
  [PASS] V3 [the sector's own stress] boosting the sector's rest-frame stress contributes at the level of its share of the mass inside the orbit, 6e-16 of the Sun, which is eleven orders below the bound on alpha_1
           (sector mass inside Saturn's orbit = 5.89e-16 solar masses; the alpha_1 bound is about 1e-4)
    Saturn's orbit is 4.62e-05 pc, so at the Cassini floor xi = 0.03 pc the screening argument is x = r/xi = 1.54e-03
    xi [pc]      x = r/xi      transmission T(x)     implied alpha_1 scale
    5e-04        9.249e-02     1.231e-04            1.231e-04
    1e-03        4.625e-02     1.592e-05            1.592e-05
    3e-03        1.542e-02     6.036e-07            6.036e-07
    1e-02        4.625e-03     1.643e-08            1.643e-08
    3e-02        1.542e-03     6.099e-10            6.099e-10
    1e-01        4.625e-04     1.648e-11            1.648e-11
  [PASS] V4 [the scalar's leakage at the length Cassini already requires] with the screening length at its Cassini floor the transmission into Saturn's orbit is 6e-10, so the scalar's contribution to the preferred-frame amplitude is six orders below the alpha_1 bound: the gate is cleared by the same length that the static test already fixes
           (T = 6.10e-10 at xi = 0.03 pc, against the alpha_1 bound of about 1e-4; the transmission falls as (r/xi)^3/6, so it is a steep function of the length)
  [PASS] V5 [WHICH BOUND BINDS, computed] the preferred-frame bound requires a screening length above 5e-4 pc while the static Cassini bound on gamma requires above 9e-4 pc: the static test is the more demanding of the two by a factor of two, so alpha_1 is satisfied automatically wherever gamma is, and the programme's existing floor of 0.03 pc clears both by more than an order of magnitude in length
           (alpha_1 needs xi > 5.37e-04 pc; gamma needs xi > 8.83e-04 pc; the programme's floor is 0.03 pc, which exceeds the binding one by a factor 34)
  [PASS] V6 [THE GATE] taking the two contributions together -- the sector's boosted stress at 1e-15 and the scalar's leakage at 6e-10, against a bound of 1e-4 -- the preferred-frame gate is cleared, and it is cleared by structure rather than by tuning: the tilt generates no background MOND gradient at all, and what leakage remains is screened by the same length the static test already fixes
           (the larger of the two contributions is 6.10e-10, which is 2e+05 times below the bound)
    READING: the gate is closed, and the reason is the structural fact in V1. A boost tilts the clock's normal and the background scalar's gradient TOGETHER, because
    the scalar depends on the clock alone, so no orthogonal gradient is generated and there is no first-order preferred-frame coupling to screen. What remains is the
    scalar's ordinary leakage into the solar system, and that is governed by the same screening length the Cassini measurement of gamma already bounds -- more tightly
    than the preferred-frame measurement does. This is why the earlier action died and this one does not: there the clock was coupled to matter directly.
    LIMITS: the leakage estimate assumes the preferred-frame amplitude is controlled by the same double-filter transmission as the static one, which is the same scalar
    reaching the same place but is an assumption rather than a derivation; the screening floor 0.03 pc is this programme's own; the sector's local density is the standard
    0.4 GeV/cm^3 scaled by the retained fraction; no full post-Newtonian parameter is extracted from a metric expansion, and none is claimed to be.

L204 COMPLETE: 6/6 checks PASS.
